{0 Type theory of the CommonMark AST}

@ai-disclosure "autonomous"
@date 2026-05-21

{1 Goal}

The {!Oymarkit_.Block} type admits values that the CommonMark
parser will never produce. For example, the AST permits a [Blocks]
splicing node nested inside another [Blocks] node, or a [Heading]
whose [level] field is [42]. The parser produces a strict subset of
[Block.t]; we call this subset the {e well-typed} ASTs.

Our goal is to characterize that subset {b empirically} — by
generating broadly, running a battery of properties, shrinking each
counterexample, and tightening the generator until [Property.all]
holds.

The discipline that emerges is the type theory of the CommonMark AST
as observed through this implementation.

{1 Why "type theory"?}

The CommonMark specification is operational: it defines a parser, not a
grammar over the AST. The AST type {!Oymarkit_.Block.t} is therefore
permissive — it admits "ill-typed" values that no parse can produce.

A typing discipline carves out the well-typed subset. In principle it
could live in three places:

{ol
{- {b In the type.} Refine constructors (e.g. [Heading] could carry a
   [level : (int, 1..6) refinement]). OCaml's surface syntax limits us.}
{- {b In a generator.} The shape of the generator is the type discipline
   made syntactic — every constraint encoded in the generator is a rule.}
{- {b In a predicate table.} Named, declarative checks lifted over the
   tree. Useful for {e reporting} which rule was violated.}}

We use (2). The generator builds [Block.t] directly via the smart
constructors in {!Oymarkit_.Block} and {!Oymarkit_.Inline}, so every
sample is well-formed at the type level. Constraints beyond what the
smart constructors enforce (heading levels in [1..6], non-empty
paragraphs, etc.) are encoded by the {e shape} of the generator. There
is no surface AST and no rule table.

{1 Architecture}

{[
                Gen_block.gen_block
                       |
                       v
                Oymarkit_.Block.t
                       |
                       v
                Property.{roundtrip, uniformity_*, ...}
                       |
                       v
                Pass | Fail { reason; expected; actual }
]}

The pipeline lives in [src/property/]:

{ul
{- {!Oymarkit_property.Gen_block.gen_block} — a [QCheck2.Gen.t] over
   [Block.t]. Size-bounded with integrated tree-shrinking; uses a
   fixed vocabulary for inline text to keep counterexamples readable.}
{- {!Oymarkit_property.Property.all} — the property battery
   ([roundtrip], [normalize_idempotent], [render_determinism],
   [uniformity_block_quote], [uniformity_list_item]).}}

{1 Workflow}

The loop is:

{ol
{- {b Generate.} Sample a [Block.t] from the current [gen_block]. The
   generator's current shape is the conjectured typing discipline.}
{- {b Check.} Run every [Property.t] in [Property.all] on the block.}
{- {b Shrink.} On any failure, QCheck2 shrinks to a minimal
   counterexample (integrated shrinking — no separate shrinker to
   maintain).}
{- {b Diagnose.} Read the shrunk witness; identify the typing rule it
   violates. Examples we expect to see:
   {ul
   {- A paragraph beginning with [- ] inside a list item reparses as a
     nested list.}
   {- A thematic break of [-] characters inside a [Ulist] item collides
     with the list marker.}
   {- A code block whose info string contains spaces.}}}
{- {b Promote.} Encode the rule by tightening [gen_block] so it can no
   longer produce such samples.}
{- {b Repeat} until [Property.all] holds at large [count]. The
   surviving shape of [gen_block] {b is} the typing discipline.}}

{1 What "equal" means}

[Property.canonical] is the equality used by every check.

It is currently {!Oymarkit_.Pp.pp_block} applied to the
{{!Oymarkit_.Block.normalize}normalized} block. Two blocks are equal
iff their canonical forms are equal strings.

This is deliberately lossy: it drops [Meta.t], drops most [Layout]
fields, and truncates inline previews. The {e right} granularity for
discovering structural rules; the {e wrong} granularity for
layout-preservation bugs. Sharpen when needed.

{1 What this is not}

{ul
{- {b A spec.} The rules encoded in [gen_block] describe this
   implementation's behavior, not the CommonMark spec. They are likely
   a superset of any clean formal characterization.}
{- {b A round-trip fixer.} When a counterexample shows that
   [parse (render b) /= b], the rule we add says "don't generate such
   [b]" — we do not change the parser or renderer to make it round-trip.}
{- {b Exhaustive.} The generator is intentionally narrow. Extensions
   (math, tables, footnotes), HTML, links, and setext headings are
   omitted from v1 and will be added once the core is stable.}}

{1 Open directions}

{ul
{- {b Sharper equality.} A structural [Block.equal_ignoring_meta] that
   does not go through [Pp]'s lossy preview.}
{- {b Inline-level properties.} Currently only block-level
   round-tripping is checked. Inline-level analogues should follow.}
{- {b Container battery.} [uniformity_*] is currently defined for
   block quote and unordered list. Add ordered list, nested
   containers, and an [n]-deep wrapping variant.}
{- {b Rule provenance.} Each shrunk counterexample should be recorded
   alongside the generator tightening it motivated, so we can recover
   the minimal witnesses behind each rule.}}


{1 2026-05-27 Edit}

When a test fails, it can also indicates our notion of equality / equivalence needs to be changed / updated.

Every tightening of the generator needs a rationale.

Correction on tightening generator: we want the most flexible generator. We want to generate generators (parametrize generator over weights or boolean flags). Whenever we add a new rule that encodes a piece of knowledge, we want to make sure we have a generator that can generate both the example and counter-example of that rule.
